{"ts":"2026-11-15T00:59:51.908Z","actor":"claude","stage":"factcheck","action":"factcheck.review","details":{"verdict":"pass","notes":"NASA (lunar laser ranging page): 3.8 cm/yr, 'the rate that fingernails grow'; ~2.5 s laser round trip; distance to within a few millimetres; Apollo reflectors (the photo is Apollo 14's, as14-67-09386). Computed: 1971 to 2026 is 55 years x 3.8 cm = 209 cm, 'about two metres' (a constant rate is a fine approximation over 55 years; NASA notes the drift is slowing over geological time). NASA Tidal Locking: the energy comes primarily from Earth's oceans, which bulge and exert their own pull; I avoided mechanism details NASA doesn't state. NASA Eclipses: too small to cover the Sun after another 600-million-plus years. The eclipse image is the 2017 total eclipse (diamond ring)."},"gitSha":"910e1db"}
